  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4(\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x+1)=3\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x+1) & =3\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x+4 & =3\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}\u2061x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(-\\frac{1}{4})=-0.244\\,978\u2248-0.245\\,0\n$$\n\nSince $-0.245\\,0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.245\\,0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi -0.245\\,0\u22482.896\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-0.245\\,0,2.896\\,6.$\n\nFinally, the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n(-0.245\\,0)\\cdot 2.896\\,6\u2248-0.7\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
